---
import Agents from "../components/Agents.astro";
import Burrow from "../components/Burrow.astro";
import Collections from "../components/Collections.astro";
import Compare from "../components/Compare.astro";
import Demo from "../components/Demo.astro";
import Download from "../components/Download.astro";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import Features from "../components/Features.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Outro from "../components/Outro.astro";
import Pricing from "../components/Pricing.astro";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { shots } from "../shots";
---

<Base>
  <Nav />
  <main>
    <Hero />

    <Compare
      id="features"
      shot={shots.highlight}
      alt="QRabbit with a passage selected in the paper and the Explore toolbar above it, and an earlier thread's highlight further up the page."
    >
      <Fragment slot="title">No more copying text. <em>Just highlight it.</em></Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="lede">
        Select a passage and choose Explore. The thread opens beside the paper with the passage and the text around it
        already in hand, and the highlight stays in the paper as your way back to it.
      </Fragment>
      <div class="u">So how do the four value functions relate?</div>
      <div class="a">
        The optimal ones are the best the on-policy ones can be, <i>V</i>*(<i>s</i>) = max<sub>π</sub>
        <i>V</i><sup>π</sup>(<i>s</i>), and the same holds for <i>Q</i>. Each can be written in terms of the other…
      </div>
      <div class="keys"><kbd>Ctrl</kbd><kbd>C</kbd> in the paper → <kbd>Alt</kbd><kbd>Tab</kbd> → <kbd>Ctrl</kbd><kbd>V</kbd></div>
      <div class="u paste">
        <q>In deep RL, we deal with parameterized policies: policies whose outputs are computable functions that depend on a set of parameters</q>
        What does this mean?
      </div>
      <div class="a">Which text is this from? Without the paragraphs around it, I can only guess what the parameters are.</div>
      <div class="u lost">It's from the Spinning Up page I pasted earlier. Section 3, I think?</div>
    </Compare>

    <Compare
      shot={shots.branches}
      up="The list is three answers up"
      alt="A QRabbit thread answering 'What are the main ways to learn a good policy?' with a numbered list; two of its points are highlighted with branch markers, and the outline above lists the two branches."
    >
      <Fragment slot="title">Answers come as lists. <em>Follow-ups bury them.</em></Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="lede">
        Ask about the third point, then the fourth, and the list scrolls out of sight while the conversation wanders. In
        QRabbit each point you ask about opens its own branch, and the answer stays where it was.
      </Fragment>
      <div class="a">
        <ol start="3">
          <li><b>Actor-critic methods</b> combine the two: a critic estimates value to cut the variance…</li>
          <li><b>Model-based methods</b> learn a model of the environment and plan with it…</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="u">Can you go deeper on actor-critic?</div>
      <div class="a">
        The policy gradient weights each action by the return that followed it, which is noisy. The critic swaps that
        return for an estimate of the advantage, with far less spread…
      </div>
      <div class="u">And the model-based ones?</div>
      <div class="a">They learn the dynamics and plan with them. That saves real experience, but a wrong model compounds its errors, so…</div>
      <div class="u lost">Sorry, what was the second family again?</div>
    </Compare>

    <Burrow />
    <Demo />
    <Features />
    <Collections />
    <Agents />
    <Pricing />
    <Download />
    <Faq />
    <Outro />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>
